'''Lemerig''' is an [[Oceanic language]] spoken on [[Vanua Lava]], in [[Vanuatu]].

The language is no longer actively spoken, having [[language shift|receded in favour of]] its neighbors, including [[Vera'a language|Vera'a]] and possibly also [[Mwotlap language|Mwotlap]] or [[Vurës language|Vurës]].{{sfn|François|2012|p=87}} The two remaining speakers live on the northern coast of the island.<ref>[https://marama.huma-num.fr/AF-field.htm#Vanuatu List of Banks islands languages].</ref>

==Name==
{{Unsourced|section|date=February 2025}}
The name ''Lemerig'' refers to a now abandoned village in northern [[Vanua Lava]]; it is spelled {{lang|lrz|Lēmērig}} {{IPA|lrz|lɪmɪˈriɣ|}} in the language of the same name. It reflects an earlier [[Proto-Torres–Banks language|Proto-Torres-Banks]] form ''*lemeriɣi'', where the ''*riɣi'' component likely means "small".

==Dialects==
Lemerig has sometimes been referred to using the names of its local varieties: ''Päk'',<ref group="lower-alpha">''Päk'' {{IPA|lrz|pæk|}} is a village on the north coast of Vanua Lava island, today often referred to using its [[Mwotlap language|Mwotlap]] name ''Abek'' {{IPA|mlv|aᵐbɛk|}} (with locative). Due to its vowel {{IPA|[æ]}} (potentially ambiguous to Western ears), the original name in Lemerig was spelled ''Pak'' in [[#RHC|Codrington 1885]] ([https://archive.org/details/melanesianlangua00codruoft/page/39/mode/2up?view=theater pp.39 sqq]), but ''Pek'' in [[#RHC_tM|Codrington 1891]] ([https://archive.org/details/melanesiansstudi00codruoft/page/81/mode/1up?q=Pek p.81]).</ref> ''Sasar'', and ''Alo-Teqel''.

Judging from wordlists published by missionary and linguist [[Robert Henry Codrington|Robert Codrington]],<ref>See [[#RHC|Codrington 1885]], [https://archive.org/details/melanesianlangua00codruoft/page/39/mode/1up pp.39-52 sqq.].</ref> these three varieties were very close to each other. The little differences there were went extinct during the 20th century.

